// A typed hole in pattern position: an arm you have not finished writing.
//
// `?` is already the expression hole -- a term with a typing rule rather than
// a parse failure with a placeholder (12). In a *pattern* slot the same token
// spells the same idea one level down: not a value you have not written, but a
// **test** you have not written. `case c { Red => ret 0, ? => ret 1, ... }`
// says "when `c` is `Red`, answer 0; and there is a second branch here whose
// pattern I have not decided yet".
//
// The consequence is the part worth learning, and it is not the obvious one.
// An unfinished test is neither satisfied nor refuted, so a scrutinee that
// reaches it **stops**. It does not fall through to the arms written after it,
// because whether the hole would have matched first is exactly what has not
// been decided -- and it does not take the hole's own arm either, because a
// hole is not a wildcard. Reading it either way would have the machine guess,
// and a guess here is a wrong answer that looks like a right one.
//
// So a hole in a match partitions the constructors into two groups: the ones
// settled *before* it, which run exactly as they always did, and the ones it
// stands in front of, which are held until you finish the pattern.
data Color : Type {
  Red : Color;
  Green : Color;
  Blue : Color;
}

// `Red` is settled by its own arm, which precedes the hole -- so this function
// answers `0` for `Red` today, unfinished match and all. `Green` and `Blue`
// both reach the hole and stop; the pathological tree carries those two,
// because a stopped program is a testing artifact rather than a lesson.
def classify(c : Color) -> F Integer {
  case c {
    Red => ret 0,
    ? => ret 1,
    Blue => ret 2
  }
}

// A hole can also sit *inside* a constructor pattern, where it leaves the head
// alone and unfinishes only the payload. `Just(?)` cannot decide a `Just` --
// the payload test is missing -- but it refutes a `Nothing` outright, because
// the head is written. That is why `describe` still answers for `Nothing`.
data Maybe : Type {
  Nothing : Maybe;
  Just : (x : Integer) --> Maybe;
}

def describe(m : Maybe) -> F Integer {
  case m {
    Just(?) => ret 100,
    Nothing => ret 200
  }
}

// Filling a hole is an ordinary edit to the source, not a mode change: the arm
// was already a real arm, so writing `Green` where the `?` is turns the two
// held constructors into a settled one and a genuinely uncovered one. Nothing
// about the rest of the file has to be re-read to do it.
describe(Nothing)

// The harness checks. `expect: goal` rather than `expect: clean`, and the
// difference is the lesson repeated once more: a hole is reported as a GOAL --
// the thing the machine needs you to provide -- not as an error, and a program
// carrying one is unfinished rather than wrong.
//@ expect: goal
//@ expect-def: classify
//@ expect-def: describe
//@ expect-last-value: 200
